uery fetches related data, but implementing efficient resolvers is harder—N+1 queries lurk easily. Caching moves from HTTP to application layer (more work). Better for mobile, varied clients, or when query patterns are unknown upfront.\n\n**Call it**: REST if your API surfaces a few stable resources and clients are homogeneous (server → server, standard web app). GraphQL if clients are diverse, bandwidth matters (mobile), or they'll request unpredictable field combos. GraphQL costs more to build right; REST is slower to query but faster to ship.","ttft_ms":3953,"type":"result","duration_ms":6767,"uuid":"1f569b80-7c53-4e04-8873-4a958c3c08cf","ttft_stream_ms":772,"time_to_request_ms":122,"first_content_frame_ms":772,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":479,"cacheReadInputTokens":7236,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0031286,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":279,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"credential_expires_at":"2026-12-12T05:14:51.381Z"}